  "articles": [
    {
      "title": "About the syllabary",
      "body": "<p>Sequoyah, a Cherokee silversmith who did not read English, created the syllabary in the 1810s. After about twelve years of work he showed it to the Cherokee Nation in 1821, and within a few years much of the nation could read and write it. A Cherokee newspaper, the <i>Cherokee Phoenix</i>, began printing in 1828.</p>\n<p>Each character stands for a whole syllable, not a single sound. Some of the shapes were taken from Latin letters Sequoyah had seen, but with new values, so Ꭰ is a, Ꮃ is la and Ꮢ is sv.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Reading the chart",
      "body": "<p>The chart has six columns, one for each vowel: a, e, i, o, u and v. Each row is a consonant: ga, ha, la, ma and so on. A few rows have extra characters: ka beside ga, hna and nah in the n row, the lone s, and dla beside tla.</p>\n<p>Cherokee does not show every sound in writing. Vowel length, tone and the glottal stop are left out, and the soft and breathy forms of g/k, d/t and dl/tl are only partly marked. Readers rely on knowing the words, the same way English readers know how to say read in two ways.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Consonant clusters",
      "body": "<p>Because every character ends in a vowel, Cherokee writes a consonant cluster by choosing a syllable whose vowel is not said. <span lang=\"chr\">ᏍᎪᎯ</span> (sgohi, ten) uses the lone s Ꮝ; other words use a whole syllable and drop its vowel in speech. Dictionaries give the exact pronunciation in a phonetic spelling alongside the syllabary.</p>"
    }
  ]
